## 1. Introduction
Exploring alternative renewable energy sources for fossil fuels is key to meeting the challenges of the energy crisis and environmental issues [1–4]. Methanol is well accepted as an excellent energy carrier for future energy systems due to its high energy density of $15.6~\mathrm{MJ/L}$ as well as its easy-to-transport and storable features [5–6]. The chemical energy of methanol can be efficiently converted into electricity by direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs), which provide renewably-generated power for automobiles and portable electronics [7–10]. However, the sluggish kinetics of the methanol oxidation reaction (MOR), the anodic reaction of DMFCs, has become a major obstacle to the commercialization of DMFCs [11]. Noble metal-based (e.g. Pt, Pd, Ru) materials are widely recognized as the most effective MOR catalysts [12–18], but the scarcity, high costs, and ease to be poisoned by CO intermediate have seriously hindered their wide application. Thus, the development of cost-effective MOR catalysts with high efficiency and long-term durability remains a challenge.
Transition metal materials, especially Ni- and Co-based species [19–21], show grand potential as MOR catalysts due to their attractive electrochemical properties and high cost-effectiveness, but their efficiency, conductivity, and stability are currently insufficient for practical application. Inspired by natural enzymatic reactions, which usually evolve complex networks to facilitate substrate transport between isolated active sites, the subtle design of interconnected porous network architectures has become an attractive strategy to overcome the inherent limitations of traditional electrocatalysts [22]. For example, the recently developed metal/metal oxide-based nanowire networks and cross-coupled carbon networks are capable to increase the active surface area and accelerate the electron/ion migration, thus greatly improving their efficiency in electrocatalysis as well as electrochemical energy storage [23–25]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), intrinsically porous materials with tunable structures and isolated active sites, have recently emerged as promising skeleton units for contracting hierarchical network architectures [26–28]. However, there has been ample research on electrocatalysts of MOFs and their derivatives, but investigation of MOF-based networks is actually limited and their electrocatalytic performance toward MOR is still not optimized [29].
Recently, sophisticated defect engineering techniques such as phosphorous (P) doping [30,31] and amorphization [32] have been reported to further reinforce the electrocatalytic performance. P doping is demonstrated as a facile and effective method to improve the activity, conductivity, and long-term stability of electrochemical materials by forming PAC and PAO bonds [33–35]. The amorphization of crystalline materials can enhance the activity and structural durability of electrocatalysts by endowing them with unique amorphous properties such as abundant defects, isotropy, absence of grain boundaries, and flexibility [36]. Therefore, it is highly desired to fabricate a unique network structure by delicate defect engineering to boost MOR efficiency.
Here, we report a two-step pyrolysis strategy of heat-induced interconnection (HII) and heat-induced phosphorization (HIP) to fabricate directly interconnected networks of amorphous NiCo MOF nanowires with $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ doping (a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P) as efficient MOR electrocatalyst. The HII process promoted the formation of the directly interconnected network from NiCo-MOF nanowires with durable self-supporting porous structure, enlarged charge transport path, and tailored amorphous property, while the HIP process optimized the local electronic density of active metal sites for enhancing the intrinsic electrocatalytic efficiency. The obtained a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P electrocatalyst exhibited superior activity and excellent stability for long-term MOR measurements. This work provides critical insights into designing amorphous MOF-based electrocatalysts with stable porous structures and adjustable defects to accelerate various electrochemical reactions.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $298\ \%$ ) was purchased from Beijing Innochem Technology Co., ltd, China. Cobalt (II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O},\geq99\%)$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $296\ \%$ ), potassium hydroxide (KOH), $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Biphenyldicar boxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}B P D C}$ , $297\ \%$ ), sodium hypophosphite $(\mathsf{N a H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{2}$ , $298\ \%$ and methanol $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $299.5\ \%$ ) were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., ltd, China. All the above chemicals were directly used without further purification.
## 2.2. Synthesis of NiCo-MOFNWs
The NiCo-MOFNWs were prepared by a hydrothermal treatment [37]. Typically, ${\sf N i}({\sf N O}_{3})_{2}.6{\sf H}_{2}0$ $\left(0.33\mathrm{\mmol}\right)$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{-}$ $.6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{\langle1.67~mmol\rangle}$ were mixed into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water, then NaOH (4 mmol) and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P D C}$ (2 mmol) was dissolved in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water. The separate solutions were mixed into a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ Teflon reactor under continuous stirring for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ and then heated at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resulting products were collected by centrifugation, washed with deionized water and ethanol three times, and finally dried under freeze-drying.
