## 2.4. Synthesis of $a$ -NiCo-MOFNWs-P
For comparison, the a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h sample was prepared through the same method of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P except that the HIP heating time was expanded to $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ Besides, NiCoMOFNWs-P was also prepared under the same reaction condition of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P except that NiCo-MOFNWs $(30\ \mathrm{mg})$ was used instead of a-NiCo-MOFNWs.
## 2.5. Characterization
## 2.6. Electrochemical measurement
The electrochemical measurements were conducted in a threeelectrode electrochemical cell using a graphite rod as the counter electrode and $\sf{A g/A g C l}$ as the reference electrode through a Princeton PMC 2000&1000 electrochemical workstation [38]. The catalyst ink was prepared by mixing $2{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of catalyst, $495~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of water, $495~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of ethanol and $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5\mathrm{\wt\%}$ Nafion solution followed by ultrasonication for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, $7~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was loaded onto the glassy carbon electrode and $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the ink was loaded onto the nickel foam $(1\ \mathrm{cm}\ \times\ 1\ \mathrm{cm})$ , which was prepared as the working electrode respectively with the loading amount of $0.2~\mathrm{mg~cm}^{-2}$ . The electrolyte was the aqueous solution containing $1\mathrm{~M~KOH}$ and $1\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol. The as-prepared electrode was activated by measuring CV curves for 10 cycles between 0 and $_{0.6\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs $\mathsf{A g/A g C l}$ electrode at the scanning rates of $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . The long-term stability of the catalyst was evaluated by performing 1000 cycles of CV measurements at a scan rate of $50\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation and structural characterizations of a-NiCo-MOFNWs$P$
The preparation procedure of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P is illustrated in Scheme 1. 1D NiCo-MOFNWs were prepared first via a hydrothermal method, followed by a heat-induced interconnection (HII) process to facilitate the cross-linking and amorphization of MOF nanowires, and subsequent P doping through a heatinduced phosphorization (HIP) treatment. In this approach, the HII treatment contributes to the formation of the directly interconnected networks since the high surface energy in the edge of the nanowires, and it can also induce an amorphous phase transition from the crystallinity phase [39] due to the heat-induced rapid removal of solvent from the pores of NiCo-MOFNWs and the resultant structure collapse, leading to the formation of the directly interconnected network of amorphous NiCo-MOFNWs (denoted as a-NiCo-MOFNWs). Then the HIP treatment was carried out under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere with $\mathsf{N a H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{2}$ as a phosphorus source to obtain the final P-doped network material (a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P), which is expected to further optimize the electronic structure and surface defects [30,31,40].
As shown in Fig. 1a-c, the SEM, TEM, and high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADFSTEM) images of the pristine NiCo-MOFNWs exhibit individually dispersed 1D nanowire morphology with high aspect ratio and smooth surface, and its crystalline structure is confirmed by XRD (Fig. 2a, black curve, and Fig. S1). In addition, the structure uniformity of the obtained NiCo-MOFNWs was adjusted by tuning the content ratio of Ni and Co components in the metallic precursor (Figs. S1 and S2), and an optimal $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C o}$ ratio of 1:5 was achieved. After the HII treatment of NiCo-MOFNWs at $390^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , the straight and smooth NiCo-MOF nanowires become curved and cross-linked into a directly interconnected network with self-supporting porous skeleton and extended mass transfer channels (Fig. 1d-1f). The corresponding XRD pattern shows only one broad and weak peak (Fig. 2a, red curve) instead of the typically sharp diffraction peaks of crystalline NiCo-MOF (Fig. 2a, black curve), indicating the successful formation of the amorphous phase [41,42] of a-NiCo-MOFNWs.
To obtain a direct visualization of the morphological and structural evolution of this unique amorphous network of a-NiCoMOFNWs, we monitored the HII products at different heating temperatures (Fig. S3-S5). First, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was carried out at $30\sim900^{\circ}C$ under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere to evaluate the thermal property of NiCo-MOFNWs and screen the appropriate heating temperature in the HII process. As shown in Fig. S3 and S4, the first two weight losses below $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ were mainly attributed to the removal of absorbed $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , and the coordinated solvent molecules, while the third weight loss at a temperature above $420~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ originated from the decomposition of MOF skeleton and carbon release [37]. Moreover, the addition of Ni has a certain influence on the selection of the HII temperature range: the higher the Ni content, the narrower the range of HII temperature to be selected. Based on the TGA results, we tried different HII temperatures around the temperature plateau $(350\sim450^{\circ}C)$ between the second and third weight loss stages to induce the formation of amorphous MOFs without destroying the interconnect nanowire network structure.
XRD patterns of NiCo-MOFNWs with different HII temperatures (Fig. S5a) show a structural transition from MOFs crystal to amorphous phase at around $390^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ , and further converted to the crystalline phase of $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C o}$ oxides (PDF #42–1467) at $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ This result indicates that the crystalline of NiCo-MOFNWs can be tuned by the HII treatment. The corresponding SEM images reveal that a lower HII temperature of $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$ is not sufficient to force the bending of NiCo-MOFNWs to form the interconnection network (Fig. S5b and S5c), while the cross-linked network with less homogeneous morphology could be observed at $370^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (Fig. S5d). The uniform structure of the directly interconnected nanowire network was obtained by further increasing the HII temperature to $390^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ (Fig. S5e), and excessive temperatures over $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. S5f) led to the formation of discrete nanoparticles that disrupted the structural continuity of the network. The above TGA, XRD, and SEM analysis suggest that the HII treatment can induce the bending and direct interconnection of NiCo-MOFNWs to construct the nanowire network. Besides, the uniformity and crystallinity of the a-NiCo-MOFNWs network can be adjusted by varying the HII temperature.
After the HIP treatment for P doping, the obtained a-NiCoMOFNWs-P composite successfully inherit the nanowire network structure (Fig. 1g-1i), and no obvious change is observed from the XRD pattern (Fig. 2a, blue curve). The Raman spectra (Fig. 2b and Fig. S6) further demonstrate the formation of the amorphous structure of a-NiCo-MOFNWs and a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P with rich defects [43,44]. Specifically, compared with the typical crystalline Raman peaks of the pristine NiCo-MOFNWs (Fig. 2b, Fig. S6, black curve), the emerged G-band and D-band at $1580~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1300~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ after HII (Fig. 2b, Fig. S6, red curve) and HIP (Fig. 2b, Fig. S6, blue curve) treatments indicate the formation of graphitic carbon and disordered/defect carbon, respectively, which confirms the induced structural defects in a-NiCo-MOFNWs. In addition, the $\mathsf{D}/\mathsf{G}$ intensity ratio was slightly enhanced after HIP treatment, indicating that P-doping can lead to a further increase in lattice defects. To further investigate the structural details of the as-prepared materials, high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) and energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) mapping were performed. As shown in Fig. 2c, the HRTEM and the corresponding Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) images of pristine NiCo-MOFNWs display a clear lattice spacing of $1.4\:\mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to the (101) plane of NiCo-MOF, while a-NiCo-MOFNWs exhibits a disordered structure (Fig. 2d), confirming the formation of amorphous phase during the HII treatment. Moreover, the HRTEM result of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P (Fig. 2e) confirms the reservation of the amorphous structure after P doping.
The HAADF-STEM and EDS mapping images of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P (Fig. 2f) further verify the homogeneous distribution of the P component on the skeleton of the nanowire network, indicating the successful doping of phosphorous.
The composition and surface chemical interaction of a-NiCoMOFNWs-P was studied by Fourier transformed infrared spectrometer (FTIR) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Fig. 3). As shown in Fig. 3a, NiCo-MOFNWs present the characteristic IR bands at 3606, 1597, 1392 and $810~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are assigned to the stretching vibration of $_{0-\mathrm{H}}$ , asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of $-\mathsf{C O O^{-}}$ and bending vibration of $0\mathrm{-H}$ , respectively [37,45]. After the HII treatment, the typical peaks of $^{0-\mathrm{H}}$ decrease significantly, indicating the importance of dehydroxylation in the fabrication of the directly interconnected nanowire network of a-NiCo-MOFNWs. After the HIP process, the asprepared a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P composite show additional bands at 1387 and $1057~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ related to the stretching modes of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{P=}0$ and PAO, suggesting the successful doping of the P element [46,47]. As shown in Fig. S7 and Table S1, the XPS survey further confirms the existence of the P element with an atomic percentage of $8.9\%$ .
The high-resolution XPS spectrum of $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ (Fig. 3b) in a-NiCoMOFNWs-P shows two peaks at 134.4 and $133.5\mathrm{eV}$ , which are corresponded to the PAO bond. The identical C 1 s peaks (Fig. 3c) at 284.8 and 288.6 eV for NiCo-MOFNWs, a-NiCo-MOFNWs, and aNiCo-MOFNWs-P are ascribed to the $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}-0-{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}=0$ groups [45]. Notably, a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P presents an additional weak C 1 s peak at $286.4\:\mathrm{eV}$ that can be assigned to the CAP bond, indicating that the $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ atom was doped mainly by substituting the C element. The O 1 s spectrum (Fig. 3d) of NiCo-MOFNWs exhibits typical peaks at 533.2, 532.0, and $531.4{\mathrm{~eV}}$ corresponding to the $0{\mathrm{-H}}.$ , $0{\cdot}C{=}0$ and MO (M, metal) groups, while the additional peak at $530.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ of a-NiCo-MOFNWs is assigned to the lattice $0^{2-}$ [48]. This result suggests that the dehydration and dehydroxylation process during the HII process may cause the extraction of some O species and the exposure of the lattice $0^{2-}$ . The emerging O 1 s peak at $530.8{\ \mathrm{eV}}$ in a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P can be ascribed to the $\mathsf{P}{-}0$ group that formed via $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ doping through the HIP treatment. The high-resolution Co 2p (Fig. 4a) and Ni 2p (Fig. S8) spectra of the three as-prepared samples reveal the existence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ (781.3 eV), $C o^{2+}$ $(783.5~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathsf{N i}^{3+}$ $856.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ [45], while aNiCo-MOFNWs and a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P exhibit enhanced ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}\big/$ ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ratio than that of the pristine NiCo-MOFNWs, which may be attributed to the fact that some $C o^{2+}$ lost electrons and changed to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ for charge balance during dehydroxylation in HII and the cationic doping of $\mathsf{P}^{5+}$ by replacing $C^{4+}$ in HIP (Fig. 4b).
According to the above results, it can be demonstrated that a directly interconnected network based on NiCo-MOFNWs was successfully constructed through the effects of dehydroxylation in the HII treatment, which also introduce numerous defects into the NiCo-MOFNWs due to the amorphization of NiCo-MOFNWs. Besides, P doping further optimized the electronic structure of active metal sites through doping behavior by replacing C atoms via the HIP process.
## 3.2. Evaluation of electrocatalytic MOR performance
The electrocatalytic MOR performance of the as-prepared samples was carried out in a standard three-electrode system containing an alkaline electrolyte (Fig. 5a) [38]. As shown in Fig. S9, the CV curves of initial NiCo-MOFNWs with different $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C o}$ ratios were tested, and the sample with a $\mathsf{N i}/\mathsf{C o}$ elemental ratio of 1:5 shows the highest current density at $0.6\mathrm{V}.$ Fixed on this elemental ratio, the subsequent a-NiCo-MOFNWs and a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P samples were prepared. Fig. 5b displays the CV curves of as-prepared aNiCo-MOFNWs-P coated on a glassy carbon electrode that tested at a scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , in 1 M KOH and a mixture of $1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH and $1\mathrm{~M~}$ methanol, respectively. Once methanol was added to the KOH electrolyte, the current density of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P exhibits a sharp increase, indicating the predominance of the MOR process in the electrocatalytic measurement. Then the as-prepared electrocatalysts were loaded on nickel foam (NF) to promote the diffusion of bubbles produced during the MOR process. As shown in Fig. 5c, a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P exhibits a superior electrocatalytic activity $(\mathrm{j}_{0.6\ \vee}=216.8\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2};$ than those of NiCo-MOFNWs, a
NiCo-MOFNWs, and blank NF, revealing the optimal morphological and structural advantages in electro-driven MOR. The calculated mass activity and specific activity of the catalysts (Fig. 5d) further confirm the performance superiority of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P, which surpasses that of most previously reported noble-metal-free electrocatalysts (Table S2). The electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of the as-prepared catalysts was evaluated from the electrochemical double-layer capacitance using the CV method at different scan rates in the non-faradic potential range of $0.15\substack{-0.25\mathrm{V}}$ vs Ag/AgCl. As shown in Fig. S10, the estimated double-layer capacitance of aNiCo-MOFNWs-P is $12.84\mathrm{mF}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is higher than that of aNiCo-MOFNWs, indicating more active sites in the a-NiCoMOFNWs-P catalyst.
In addition to the enhanced electrocatalytic activity in MOR, the a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P hybrid also exhibits long-term stability. As shown in Fig. 5e, a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P retains a high activity of 167.3 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (j0.6 V) after $1000~\mathrm{{CV}}$ cycles of MOR reaction, $77\%$ of its initial value, while the current loss of NiCo-MOFNWs under the same condition reaches almost $100\%$ (Fig. S11). After MOR, a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P maintains its original morphology (Fig. S12a), and the $\mathtt{P2p}$ XPS spectrum confirms its structural stability (Fig. S12b). Combined with the physicochemical characterization results, the enhanced MOR activity and high durability of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P can be attributed to the elongated mass transfer channel, highly exposed metal sites, and optimal P-doping amount achieved from the unique structure of P-doped directly interconnected networks of amorphous MOF nanowires.
## 3.3. Mechanistic studies of MOR over a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P
To better understand the effect of the unique interconnected network structure of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P on the catalytic performance, a control experiment was conducted on NiCo-MOFNWs-P, which was prepared by directly treating the initial NiCoMOFNWs through a HIP process, without the intermediate HII treatment. Different from the directly interconnected network structure of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P, the as-prepared NiCo-MOFNWsP shows disordered nanoparticle-agglomeration morphology (Fig. S13a) and metal phosphide composition (Fig. S13b), indicating that the HII process is crucial to constructing the directly interconnected network. Moreover, as shown in Fig. S14, the MOR performance of NiCo-MOFNWs-P decreased dramatically compared to that of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P. This result verifies the significant advantages of the directly interconnected network structure in the electrocatalytic process, which can provide a fluent channel for mass transport and boosts the catalytic kinetics, and the selfsupported frameworks can prevent the aggregation and collapse of nanostructure to enhance the catalytic durability.
The influence of P doping on the electrocatalytic performance of MOR was also investigated. In particular, the typical a-NiCoMOFNWs-P sample mentioned above has undergone a HIP treatment for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ (denoted as a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-1 h). For comparison, we additionally prepared a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h with the extended HIP treatment of $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Fig. S15). With a prolonged time of $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ doping, the FTIR spectrum of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h (Fig. S16) exhibits newly appeared peaks that can be ascribed to the stretching vibration of $\scriptstyle\mathsf{P-O-C}$ and PAOAAR (AR, aromatic ring) [49]. The XPS survey of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h (Fig. 6a, Table S1) reveals the existence of the C, P, O, Ni and Co elements, and an increased P content is clearly observed. Besides, the highresolution $\mathtt{P2p}$ spectrum of a-NiCo-MOFNWs- $.P\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{h}$ (Fig. 6b) shows an additional emerged peak that is attributed to PAC. Similarly, the PAO signal in the O 1 s spectrum (Fig. 6c) and CAP peak in the C 1 s spectrum (Fig. 6d) of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h are significantly enhanced with the increase of $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ content, indicating the doping behavior of $\mathrm{~\sf~P~}$ underwent a transformation from the dominant cationic doping (replacing $\mathsf{P}^{5+}$ to ${\mathsf{C}}^{4+}$ ) to the coexistence of cationic doping and anionic doping (replacing $\mathsf{P}^{3-}$ to $0^{2-"}$ ). Moreover, the MOF performance over a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P-1 h and a-NiCoMOFNWs- $.P{\cdot}4\mathrm{~h~}$ was further conducted. As shown in Fig. S17, a
NiCo-MOFNWs-P-4 h with increased P content exhibits a significant decrease in the MOR activity. These phenomena indicate that a small amount of P doping can benefit the electrocatalytic MOR performance, which may be originated from the formation of the PAC bond in the a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P hybrid [40,50].
The prominence of the electrocatalytic performance of a-NiCoMOFNWs-P in MOR indicates that the fabrication of directly interconnected networks with optimized P doping is an effective way in the rational design of low-dimensional MOF-based electrocatalysts, which can provide elongated channels for charge transfer/- mass diffusion and stable self-supporting structure, as well as the optimized electronic structure of metal sites for the electrocatalytic process.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, a unique porous network of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P has been developed by sequential heat-induced interconnection (HII) and heat-induced phosphorization (HIP) treatments as efficient MOR electrocatalysts. Benefiting from the structural advantages of interconnected network and P doping, the resultant aNiCo-MOFNWs-P catalyst exhibits enhanced electrocatalytic activity, kinetics, and long-term stability during the MOR process. Systematic structural and compositional characterizations unveil the successful formation of the directly interconnected network of amorphous MOFs with P doping. Combining electrochemical measurements, the effect of the unique structure on MOR is presented. The interconnected network of amorphous MOFs helps in defect enrichment, charge transport enhancement, and stability improvement, while the P-doping optimizes the local electronic density of active metal sites for enhancing the intrinsic electrocatalytic efficiency. Furthermore, the network assembly and structural evolution of a-NiCo-MOFNWs-P are presented. This work paves the way for developing amorphous MOF networks as efficient electrocatalysts with high activity, superior conductivity, and good stability.
